<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================
    Pulse — adaptive launcher icon (API 26+)

    Android 8.0 introduced adaptive icons to solve two problems:
      1. Different launchers use different icon shapes (circle,
         rounded square, squircle, teardrop, etc.). Pre-26 icons
         had to be pre-shaped, which meant they looked wrong on
         launchers with a different mask.
      2. Icon masking, shadow, and animation are launcher-owner
         decisions. Pre-26 apps couldn't participate.

    An adaptive icon delegates both problems to the launcher by
    splitting the artwork into two layers:

      <background>  — a full-bleed fill. This layer is NOT masked
                      to the icon shape until the system composes
                      the final image. It should extend edge to
                      edge (108dp × 108dp viewport) because it
                      will be cropped to the launcher's mask shape.

      <foreground>  — the logo mark. Also drawn on a 108dp × 108dp
                      viewport, but the launcher may crop or scale
                      it. Standard safe zone is a 66dp centered
                      circle; anything outside may be masked off
                      on aggressive launchers. We keep the mark
                      inside the 66dp circle.

      <monochrome>  — optional third layer (API 33+). Used by
                      "Themed Icons" — Android recolors it with
                      the system palette. Providing this makes
                      the app adopt Material You theming on
                      Pixel/Samsung launchers without any other
                      code changes.

    Viewport math:
      - Both layers declare width=108dp, height=108dp.
      - The system applies the launcher mask (diameter ≈72dp for
        a circle) as a crop.
      - Any pixel outside the visible mask on a given launcher
        disappears. So the foreground mark lives in the central
        ~66dp to survive all shapes.

    This file is under mipmap-anydpi-v26, so it is selected
    ONLY on API 26+. API 24-25 devices fall back to the bitmap
    PNGs under mipmap-*dpi/ (created in a later step).
    ============================================================
-->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
        Background layer: solid brand color.

        Pointing at a color resource works because Android
        auto-generates a <color> drawable from any @color
        reference. If we later want a gradient background, we
        would swap this for a drawable (e.g.
        @drawable/ic_launcher_background) and remove the color
        reference — but a solid brand color is the correct choice
        for a modern icon: gradients turn muddy at small sizes,
        and the Pulse brand accent reads distinctly against
        both light and dark home-screen wallpapers.
    -->
    <background android:drawable="@color/pulse_accent" />

    <!--
        Foreground layer: white "P" monogram.

        Deliberately minimal. At 66dp effective size the mark
        must be recognizable in under a second while scrolling
        through a home screen. A stylized "P" in a geometric
        sans-serif weight reads instantly at every size, in
        every launcher mask, on every wallpaper — which is the
        entire job of a launcher icon.

        Artwork is in drawable/ic_launcher_foreground.xml
        (created next).
    -->
    <foreground android:drawable="@drawable/ic_launcher_foreground" />

    <!--
        Monochrome layer: API 33+ Material You themed icon.

        Same vector as the foreground, but referenced with an
        android:tintless background so the system can apply its
        own palette. On launchers that support Themed Icons
        (Pixel default, Samsung OneUI 5+, most AOSP-based), the
        user sees the Pulse "P" tinted to match their wallpaper
        palette — while the actual icon stays branded on
        launchers that don't.

        If you ever change the foreground artwork, mirror the
        change here. This is the one place where two references
        to the same file is not duplication — it is required
        because the two layers serve different purposes (one is
        full-color artwork, one is a monochrome template).
    -->
    <monochrome android:drawable="@drawable/ic_launcher_foreground" />

</adaptive-icon>
